Grep Basic

grep --> Used for Searching in file or files

-grep "boo" a_file
    String to be searched filename

-grep -n "boo" a_file
    Used to show line numbers

-grep -v "boo" a_file
    Used for showing lines where the stirng is not matched

-grep -nv "boo" a_file
    Cascaded flags

-grep -c "boo" a_file
    shows number of line where string is matched 

-grep -l "boo" *
    flag to search for file name where string is matched and (*) means 0 or more

-grep -i "Boo" a_file
    case sensetivity is truned off

-grep -x "boo" a_file
    Used to match string, substring will not be returned

-grep -A2 "match" a_file
    A is used to display more words after matched substring 
    2 is the number of more lines to be displayed after match

-grep "e$" a_file
    Shows string ending with the char 'e'

-grep "^b" a_file
    Shows String starting with the char 'b'

-grep "oo.." a_file
    Exactly or more than 2 char after "oo"

-grep -E "bool mach" a_file
    -E flag to use complex regular expressions
    "bool mach" is matches boo or mach in the file